# Installer, uninstaller and systemd unit

- Windows is for development only. The daemon warns at startup that data-directory ACLs are unchecked on Windows.
- `scripts/install.sh` runs as root on other people's Ubuntu/Debian hosts (amd64, arm64, systemd), usually piped from curl: no prompts, everything in functions with one grouped call on the last line (a truncated download must be a syntax error), commands that could read stdin get `</dev/null`. Its `readonly RELEASE_VERSION=''` line is stamped by `scripts/release.sh`; keep it exactly that.
- Order: validate options, then every host check (root, OS, arch, systemd, ports, Docker state, download, SHA-256, archive contents, binary runs and reports the expected version) before the first change. A failed download or checksum must leave the host untouched. Payloads come from a release download (`SHA256SUMS` or `--sha256`), `--archive` (`--sha256` or a `SHA256SUMS` next to it) or `--binary` (unit from `deploy/`, uninstaller from `scripts/`); all are copied into a private `mktemp -d` and installed from there. Archives must hold exactly `LICENSE golem-deploy golem-deploy.service uninstall.sh` as plain files; modes are always set by the installer, never taken from the archive. `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL` (https or file) replaces the download directory. curl: `--proto =https --proto-redir =https --tlsv1.2`, retries, size caps.
- Docker: a working Docker is kept. Missing: Docker's apt repository per Docker's install pages (`/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc`, deb822 `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources` with `Signed-By`, fingerprint check with gpg when present), packages `docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin`, an existing repository is reused. Stopped `docker-ce`: start it. Conflicting distribution packages with no working Docker: stop with the `apt-get remove` command, never remove packages. Missing buildx: install `docker-buildx-plugin` only with `docker-ce` and Docker's repository. `--no-docker` disables all installs. Snap and rootless Docker stay unsupported. Keep the conflict list and steps in sync with Docker's pages.
- Idempotent: a re-run replaces binary, unit, uninstaller and TLS copies, rewrites `/etc/sysctl.d/60-golem-deploy.conf` (never lowering a higher value; verify by reading `/proc/sys`, since `sysctl -p` exits 0 on refusal), resets the unit's start limit and restarts; it never rewrites an existing env file or touches an existing data directory (it refuses one with the wrong owner or mode).
- A fresh install with neither listen override nor TLS files uses 443 plus an HTTP listener on 80 when both are free, otherwise a loopback port. Without `--public-host`, a public outgoing address from `ip route get` becomes `GOLEM_DEPLOY_PUBLIC_HOST`; private addresses leave it empty with a hint. The env file holds only `GOLEM_DEPLOY_*` variables of `serve` (`cmd/golem-deploy/main.go`). When a serve flag, its default, the data directory rules (`internal/datadir`), the plaintext/loopback rule or the port-range rules change, update the script's validation, its printed next steps and `docs/INSTALL.md`.
- Next steps come from the effective env file. The first admin step relies on `golem-deploy admin setup-link [--data-dir] [--origin] [--ttl]` (one line `<origin>/setup#t=<token>` on stdout; exit 0, 3 admin exists, 1 error, 2 usage or an older binary), run as the service user after the service started. The link is a secret: keep it in a variable, never in a file or the journal. Fallback: the `admin create` command (must match its flags, run as the service user). The managed-TLS fingerprint is read from this start's journal line `panel self-signed TLS fingerprint` (`internal/certs/panel.go`); keep the parser in sync. It never changes firewall rules; with ufw active it prints `ufw allow` lines.
- `scripts/uninstall.sh` (installed as `/usr/local/sbin/golem-deploy-uninstall`) surveys first, then removes service, unit, binary, cache, sysctl file and itself; `--purge` also removes Docker objects (managed label, plus the instance label read with `sqlite3` from the database when possible; containers also need the `inspector-hub` label key that every daemon-created container has and images never carry), the data and settings directories and the user. It prints what it will delete and needs a typed `purge` on a terminal or `--yes`. Never prune, never remove Docker, its repository or packages, never delete a data directory other than `/var/lib/golem-deploy`. It must cope with half-removed installations. Keep its labels in sync with `internal/docker/validate.go`.
- The unit's hardening must stay compatible with what the daemon does: run `git`, `git-lfs`, `ssh`, the docker CLI (including `docker build` through the buildx plugin, whose build client config and state are under `<data-dir>/docker-build` and scratch files in the private `/tmp`; the build runs in dockerd) and itself (askpass); connect to `/var/run/docker.sock`; bind TCP/UDP to probe game ports; write only below the data directory (plus the non-build `DOCKER_CONFIG` in the cache directory); pass env files to docker as an inherited pipe (`/dev/fd/3`). Every directive has a comment saying why; drop a directive rather than leave it unexplained. Paths handed to dockerd (bind sources) must be the real host paths, so no `TemporaryFileSystem=`/`BindPaths=` over the data directory and no `/tmp` paths. Game containers live in dockerd's cgroup: stopping the unit must never stop them, and `TimeoutStopSec` must cover the daemon's 20-second drain and store close (`internal/httpapi/server.go`, `internal/daemon/daemon.go`). The unit grants only `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`.
- Verification: `shellcheck scripts/*.sh` (CI job "Shell"); `systemd-analyze verify` on a changed unit; end to end in systemd-booted, privileged `ubuntu:24.04` and `debian:12` containers with volumes for the nested `/var/lib/docker` and `/var/lib/containerd` (the installer installs Docker inside), installing from `scripts/release.sh` output through `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL=file://...`: checksum mismatch, truncated or tampered archives, Docker missing, `--no-docker`, re-runs, an existing env file, loopback and network listeners, every setup-link exit code (a small stand-in executable can force them), `--binary`, `--archive`, uninstall, `--purge` with and without a terminal (`script -qec` gives one), and a half-removed installation. Name test objects with a unique prefix, remove exactly those afterwards and never prune.
